      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "# Getting Started with CloudScale Stream API\n\n## Prerequisites and API Key Setup\nBefore writing code, you must retrieve your unique access credentials from the CloudScale Developer Portal. Log in to your account, navigate to the **API Settings** section, and click **Generate Key** if you haven't already. Ensure you copy the resulting string immediately, as it cannot be retrieved again once closed or revoked.\n\n## Environment Configuration\nTo ensure your secrets remain secure, always inject the API key via an environment variable named `CLOUDSCALE_KEY` rather than hardcoding it. On Linux and macOS, create a file named `.env.local` in your project root, while on Windows use `.env`. Add the following line:\n```bash\nCLOUDSCALE_KEY=your_actual_api_key_here\n```\nNext, initialize your environment in your project (using `.env`) and verify that the variable is accessible in your terminal to confirm the file has been loaded correctly.\n\n## Making Your First Request\nWith your key prepared and your environment configured, you can authenticate against the Stream API using either the REST client or the provided SDK. Below is a standard GET request example using Python that queries the specific stream ID `user-123`.",
